I was talking about AI today and how easy it’s getting to go from an idea to trying it out without sitting down at a computer. I can type a few lines or talk through what I’m thinking while the idea is still taking shape. In some ways, that’s already the interface. I wrote about [dictating prompts to coding agents](/posts/20260420-let-ai-worry-about-the-code/) earlier this year. I like being able to start before I’ve worked out the whole sentence and change my mind halfway through it.

Soon after that conversation, I came across Lucas F. Costa’s 2018 essay [*In Praise of Plain Text*](https://www.lucasfcosta.com/blog/In-Praise-of-Plaintext). He was writing about what you could do with text and a Unix shell. I started chatting with my computer about how that compares with what I’m doing now, and we followed a few references. I went in thinking about text and voice. I came out curious about what happens when you can talk and still point to the thing you’re working on. That’s how this post started.

In Microsoft Word, you can already select a passage and ask Copilot about it. The highlight tells Copilot which bit you mean. The chat pane gives you room to say why you’re asking. Some of the prototypes I found go further. They let you speak while you point or draw, then show you what the system understood.

> **tl;dr**
>
> Researchers imagined combining language and pointing decades ago. Today, Microsoft Word connects document selections to chat, and research prototypes are already testing what happens when you speak and point at the same time.

## What Unix and the Mac each got right

Near the end of [The Anti-Mac Interface](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/anti-mac-interface/), Don Gentner and Jakob Nielsen give Unix and the Macintosh their due in consecutive paragraphs. The Unix shell fit programmers who could combine commands to work with a relatively small set of object types, often plain text. The Mac fit people without a programming background who worked with a manageable number of graphical documents. The parts of each interface made sense together for the work people were doing.

The Xerox Star team laid out the tradeoff in 1982 as [“seeing and pointing versus remembering and typing”](https://guidebookgallery.org/articles/designingthestaruserinterface/). Put a document on screen and I can recognize it, point at it, and see where it goes when I move it.

Gentner and Nielsen took Apple’s Macintosh guidelines as their starting point in 1996. They asked what capabilities those rules left out and pictured an interface with language, examples, and pointing together. Everyday conversation with a computer seemed remote, so they imagined a more limited language.

Speech and pointing had already been tried together. In 1980, [Richard Bolt’s *Put-That-There*](https://doi.org/10.1145/800250.807503) let people speak and point to move shapes on a display. The words could stay simple because the gesture gave the system a way to resolve what the person meant.

{% marginnote %}A transcript can also miss [what a speaker emphasizes](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1749-818X.2009.00150.x). “Move the *heading*” and “*Move* the heading” contain the same words, and the emphasis can help a listener tell what’s being corrected.{% endmarginnote %}

A chat with a language model can look like a Unix terminal. I type a line and something comes back. With a shell command, I generally know which operation I want. In chat I can ask for a brief for people who missed a meeting and work out what belongs in it as we go. [Writing about generative AI in 2023, Nielsen](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ai-paradigm/) expected people to describe outcomes and still use graphical controls to work out the details. You can see that combination in a few different forms today.

## Word and Copilot give you two places to start

In [Microsoft 365 Copilot in Microsoft Word](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/word/copilot/chat-with-copilot-about-your-word-document), the document stays in front of you. Select a paragraph and you can ask about that paragraph in the side pane. Selection has become part of how you phrase the question. You don’t have to tell the assistant where on the page to look.

